#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4e3f525-260a-55e0-9f95-79f3101b7c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4e3f525-260a-55e0-9f95-79f3101b7c7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344847} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0dc915-7e53-5e77-89bb-a2bbfb0fa057" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344846} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac1ffb2d-009b-5532-a629-10e950239817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t^2-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac1ffb2d-009b-5532-a629-10e950239817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12t^2-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344849} :answer-field/id #uuid "81366150-616c-5287-a8f2-dc0553180241" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344848}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcffff13-cdb5-5338-a8c9-56f6f641a2ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-347193" :question/problem "The position vector $r(t)$ of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-2t,\\,{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2}-2,\\,4{t}^{3}-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
